issue:1132.body:
## 1. Summary

The Evolve LLM proxy writes one usage row per proxied completion, which is correct. But it derives the row's identity from the **run** (`invocationId: evolve:<runId>`) while holding `attemptIndex` at a constant `0`. The ledger's idempotency key is `(sessionId, invocationId, invocationKind, attemptIndex)`, so for a run that makes N model calls the first row is written and calls 2..N are discarded as if they were replays of call 1. Nothing is logged on that path, so the loss is invisible.

Measured on a real environment: a 73-call research recorded **0.83 %** of its tokens, and an 85-call research recorded **0.93 %**.

## 2. Expected behaviour

The repository already pins this contract in its own tests. `(invocationId, attemptIndex)` is the **attempt axis of one logical call**, not the identity of a run:

```ts
// services/api/src/store.test.ts:1184
test("SessionStore ignores duplicate model usage attempts but keeps retries", …
// :1220  await store.appendModelInvocationUsage(record);
// :1221  await store.appendModelInvocationUsage({ …record, id: "usage-duplicate", totalTokens: 30 });
// :1222  await store.appendModelInvocationUsage({ …record, attemptIndex: 1, id: "usage-retry", totalTokens: 4 });
// :1225  assert.deepEqual(records.map((usage) => usage.id).toSorted(), ["usage-0", "usage-retry"]);
// :1226  assert.deepEqual(records.map((usage) => usage.attemptIndex).toSorted(), [0, 1]);

// services/api/src/model-usage.test.ts:83
test("USG-005 invocation attempts keep a stable invocationId and increment attemptIndex", …
```

So: two rows that share all four key fields **must** be the same attempt of the same call, and a genuinely new call must present either a new `invocationId` or a higher `attemptIndex`. Distinct calls that differ only in their row `id` (`:259`, `randomBytes(16)`) are, by this contract, not distinguishable.

The same file already contains a writer that satisfies the contract for the very same `invocationKind`:

```ts
// services/api/src/evolution/direction-draft.ts:58-63
const id = randomUUID();
await store.appendModelInvocationUsage({
  id, invocationId: id, invocationKind: "evolve", attemptIndex: 0,
  outcome: draft ? "completed" : "failed", …
```

## 3. Actual behaviour

One row per run. Everything after the first completion is dropped in silence.

## 4. Source code

All paths relative to the repository root, quoted at main `f0efd382e`.

### 4.1 The writer builds a run-scoped key with a constant attempt index

```ts
// services/api/src/evolution/llm-proxy.ts
:232    try {
:233      const usage = await recordUsage(deps, grant, model, payload, startedAt);
:234      deps.onUsage?.(usage);
:235    } catch (error) {
:236      apiLog.warn("evolve_llm_usage_unrecorded", {          // only fires on a thrown error
:245  async function recordUsage(
:252    const reported = parseProviderUsage(payload.usage, "openai-chat-completions");
:253    const record: ModelInvocationUsage = {
:254      attemptIndex: 0,                                      // constant for every call of the run
:259      id: randomBytes(16).toString("hex"),                  // unique row id — not part of the key
:261      invocationId: `evolve:${grant.runId}`,                // one key per run
:264      invocationKind: "evolve",
:277    await deps.store.appendModelInvocationUsage(record);
```

### 4.2 The ledger drops the second call without a trace

```ts
// services/api/src/store.ts
:7634   async appendModelInvocationUsage(usage: ModelInvocationUsage): Promise<void> {
:7640     if (records.some((record) =>
:7641       record.sessionId === usage.sessionId
:7642       && record.invocationId === usage.invocationId
:7643       && record.invocationKind === usage.invocationKind
:7644       && record.attemptIndex === usage.attemptIndex)) {
:7645       return;                                            // silent: no log, no counter, no signal
:7647     records.push(this.normalizeModelInvocationUsage(usage));
:7652   async getSessionUsageSummary(sessionId: string) { … }  // summariseModelUsage counts rows as calls
```

The early `return` at `:7645` is not an error, so `llm-proxy.ts:236`'s `evolve_llm_usage_unrecorded` warning cannot fire for the calls this drops.

## 5. Reproduction

### 5.1 Observed on a real run (read-only inspection of an existing data volume)

Environment: a running ScienceDiscovery stack, Evolve search driven through the normal `/idea-tree` research path, one session, provider usage reported on every call.

| Evolve run | Proxied calls (proxy log) | Σ tokens at the proxy | Ledger rows for that `runId` | Tokens in the ledger | Share recorded |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `research-2864a8e9-ab07-4c8a-9d33-0be6f17639c3` | 73 (73 × HTTP 200, 0 non-200) | 1,752,397 (in 1,282,961 / out 469,436) | **1** | 14,621 | **0.8343 %** |
| `research-4f368ef0-e973-4b88-8806-72b2b53a0967` | 85 (85 × HTTP 200, 0 non-200) | 1,665,891 (in 1,140,337 / out 525,554) | **1** | 15,545 | **0.9331 %** |

Additional readings from the same pass:

- Each surviving row's `totalTokens` equals the **first** proxied call's total, and its `startedAt`/`finishedAt` bracket only that first call — the row kept is call 1, not an aggregate.
- `invocationKind` census of the whole ledger: 7 rows total — 5 `task`, 2 `evolve`.
- **Distinct dedup keys in the ledger: 7. Keys holding more than one row: 0.** Under this key a second row for the same run can never be written — the loss is structural, not a race.
- The engine's own state file self-reports `tokens = 1,752,397` and `1,665,891`, matching the proxy sums exactly: the spend is real and known to the engine; only the ledger is short.

### 5.2 Deterministic unit reproduction (specified, **not executed** in this pass)

No model or agent run is needed:

```ts
const key = { sessionId, invocationKind: "evolve", attemptIndex: 0 };
await store.appendModelInvocationUsage({ …key, id: "call-1", invocationId: "evolve:research-x", inputTokens: 9883, outputTokens: 4738, totalTokens: 14621 });
await store.appendModelInvocationUsage({ …key, id: "call-2", invocationId: "evolve:research-x", inputTokens: 12000, outputTokens: 6000, totalTokens: 18000 });
// Assert what §2's contract requires: two rows, one per call.
// Actual: listModelInvocationUsage returns 1 row (14,621) and the second append returns silently.
```

## 6. Scientific impact

1. **Cost of a research run is understated by two orders of magnitude.** An autonomous idea-tree search recorded 0.83 % and 0.93 % of what it spent. Any budget decision, quota, or "is this worth re-running" judgement made from `/api/sessions/{id}/usage` or the usage page is made against a number that is ~1/100 of reality.
2. **The direction of the error is silently wrong, not merely coarse.** `usageStatus: "reported"` on the surviving row asserts provider figures were captured. The row is not "an estimate" — it is call 1, labelled as the run.
3. **Per-call attribution is unrecoverable from the ledger.** Which round/candidate a token figure belongs to cannot be reconstructed, so a search cannot be compared to a cheaper alternative search on cost, and cost-per-insight cannot be computed at all.
4. **Nothing surfaces the loss.** The silent `return` produces no log line, and the only warning on this path is gated on an exception. A reader has to diff the proxy log against the ledger to notice, which is how this was found.

## 7. Suggested fix direction

Either make the call identity per call, or make the attempt axis mean what the tests say it means:

1. **Give each proxied completion its own invocation identity** (matching `direction-draft.ts:58-60`): `invocationId: \`evolve:${grant.runId}:${randomUUID()}\`` or a per-request id already available at the proxy, keeping `invocationKind: "evolve"`. The run remains addressable through the existing `runId` field (`llm-proxy.ts:269`).
2. **Or** keep the run-scoped `invocationId` and carry a per-run monotonically increasing `attemptIndex` — this needs a durable counter, so option 1 is preferable.
3. **Whichever is chosen, make the drop observable.** `store.ts:7645` should at minimum log/count a suppressed append and expose why (which key collided). A silent `return` makes a bookkeeping bug indistinguishable from correct idempotency.
4. **A regression test that pins the call, not the key**: N appends with the same run-scoped `invocationId`, distinct payloads and `attemptIndex: 0` must not collapse to one row — the counterpart to `store.test.ts:1184`, which only covers an intentional replay.

## 8. Relation to existing reports (why this is filed separately)

- **[#1109]** covers a different path and the opposite direction. It cites `services/api/src/evolution/llm-proxy.ts:245-278` as the **positive precedent** — "one `appendModelInvocationUsage` per proxied completion, keyed `invocationId: evolve:${grant.runId}`" — and uses the evolve path to argue that the native-agent path should record per turn. The word "dedup" does not appear in it, and it never claims an evolve row is dropped. This report's measurement is that the precedent it cites yields **1 row for 73 calls**, because the key the precedent reuses is also the ledger's idempotency key. Same ledger, same schema file, different defect.
- **PR #1119** (open, head `1768c9d3`) implements #1109. It changes `packages/runtime-core/src/runtime.ts`, `packages/schema/src/model-usage.ts`, `services/api/src/model-usage/index.ts` and `services/api/src/runs/index.ts`. It does **not** touch `evolution/llm-proxy.ts` or `appendModelInvocationUsage`'s guard, so this issue stands both before and after #1119 merges. If they land together, `invocationKind` will gain `delegation-track` rows and the ledger's key contract becomes load-bearing for three writers, which makes §7.3 more urgent, not less.
- **#1108** (closed by #1118), **#710/#711**, **#1125**, **#1128** were checked and share neither symptom nor root cause.
- No action on any of those issues is requested here.

## 9. Version this was measured against

- SD `main` at the time of reading: `f0efd382e5e79260386ce89674eef7af1bbc05a9` (2026-11-12T08:49:00Z).
- `services/api/src/evolution/llm-proxy.ts` blob `c447143bc7…`, 280 lines, sha256 `2f8e31af4d35db9875e52540fbe3e7cfed42389e6415f79f661d14eae85ba74e`.
- `services/api/src/store.ts` blob `baa213d4c2…`, 8132 lines, sha256 `d657e95766dd2570709ee1e9e827c525e8f1f3f60fe7da8da22b0dc376294b62`.
- Run data: existing container data volume, read-only; no agent was re-run and no model was called to produce the evidence in §5.1.

## 10. What this report does not claim

- It does not claim the run-scoped key was a mistake rather than a decision — no commit message or design note establishing intent was found, and none is asserted.
- It does not quantify historical over/under-reporting beyond the two runs above; only two Evolve runs exist on the inspected volume.
- It does not ask for backfill. The numbers for calls 2..N are not recoverable from the ledger; they exist only while the proxy log survives.

issue:58.body:
> Cross-tracking: [GitCode #101](https://gitcode.com/openJiuwen/sciencediscovery/issues/101) (Chinese counterpart of this issue).

### 🚀 Background Description

ScienceDiscovery already surfaces failures to the user — connection errors, 401/5xx request failures, settings save errors, panel render crashes, and Run/tool execution errors — via toasts, inline alerts, panel error boundaries, and Run summary text. Those surfaces currently offer only **Dismiss / Retry**. There is **no** way to copy the diagnostics that belong to that error, and **no** way to turn it into an issue from the same place.

What users actually have to do today:

1. The UI shows a short message such as `Request error: Unauthorized` or a panel `render failed`;
2. The useful evidence lives in rolling files under the data directory (default `.sciencediscovery-data/logs/` — `api.log`, `runner.log`, `run.log`). Many users do not know where that is or which slice to attach;
3. They leave the product, open GitCode or GitHub, fill a template by hand, and must remember to redact tokens, API keys, and session content.

Real failures therefore rarely become actionable issues. Maintainers get “it errored” without version, error code, run/request ids, or nearby log lines.

This request is a **user-facing feedback mechanism**, not another developer-only log sink. It complements the framework diagnostic-logging work ([GitHub #8](https://github.com/openJiuwen-ai/sciencediscovery/issues/8), GitCode [#74](https://gitcode.com/openJiuwen/sciencediscovery/issues/74)): #8/#74 explicitly **do not** add a log viewer, search, or download UI. This issue is the missing “copy key logs + file an issue” path at the error itself.

### Design Ideas

On **user-visible errors**, add two actions (copy can be refined):

1. **Copy key diagnostics**: write a redacted, size-capped bundle to the clipboard. At minimum:
   - product version / build id;
   - OS and UI locale;
   - error title and body;
   - request / run / session ids when they exist;
   - the last few service-log lines related to this failure (reuse the redaction and length caps from #8/#74; do not copy whole log files).
2. **File an issue**: open the repository’s new-issue page with title and body prefilled from that bundle. GitCode (`openJiuwen/sciencediscovery`) is the canonical tracker; GitHub (`openJiuwen-ai/sciencediscovery`) is the public mirror — offer a GitHub link or a locale-based choice. The product must **not** create the issue on the user’s behalf and must **not** upload logs to a third party.

Priority error surfaces (can ship incrementally via one shared control):

- error toasts (`apps/web/src/Toasts.tsx` — dismiss only today);
- inline settings/dialog alerts (`apps/web/src/InlineErrorAlert.tsx` — dismiss only today);
- panel error boundaries (`apps/web/src/ErrorBoundary.tsx` — `label` + `message` only today);
- user-visible Run / tool failure text;
- CLI failures that have no equivalent Web surface.

**Alternatives considered**

- Copy-only, no “file issue” action: users still have to find the repo and the template.
- In-app form posting to a private backend: extra infra and auth, out of scope.
- Dumping full log files or unredacted session / tool payloads: leaks tokens, API keys, and research content.
- Silent crash telemetry: not part of this request.

**Open questions (need a product decision before implementation)**

- Whether “File an issue” defaults to GitCode or chooses GitCode vs GitHub from the UI locale;
- Whether the bundle is assembled in the client from the already-visible error plus version info, or via a local authenticated snapshot API (unredacted log files must not be exposed over HTTP).

### Involved Public APIs

- **User-visible behavior**: add Copy / File-issue actions on error controls; do not change the meaning of existing error copy.
- **HTTP**: if the client cannot see nearby log lines, a local authenticated read-only snapshot endpoint may return the redacted fields above. Do not expose `.sciencediscovery-data/logs/` as downloadable files, and do not add an anonymous public log download.
- **Issue hosts**: only open the public GitCode / GitHub “new issue” pages with prefilled content. Do not call their write APIs and do not embed a full issue editor.

### Description of Relevance to Other Modules

- Web error UI: toasts, inline alerts, error boundaries, Run summaries;
- `packages/operational-logging` and [GitHub #8](https://github.com/openJiuwen-ai/sciencediscovery/issues/8) / [GitCode #74](https://gitcode.com/openJiuwen/sciencediscovery/issues/74): reuse existing redaction, rotation, and size caps;
- CLI: the same class of failure should yield a copyable diagnostics block;
- This does not replace the framework-internal events in #8/#74, and must not put user prompts, model I/O, or tool arguments/results into the bundle.

### Test Design and Test Plan

**Functional**

- [ ] Error toast, inline alert, and error boundary expose “Copy key diagnostics” in the failure state; the clipboard contains version + error body and does **not** contain access tokens / API keys / Bearer values;
- [ ] “File an issue” opens a new-issue URL for `openJiuwen/sciencediscovery` (and optionally the GitHub mirror) with title/body already carrying the error summary and diagnostics bundle;
- [ ] Dismissing the error does not upload logs or create an issue in the background;
- [ ] CLI user-visible failures print or otherwise provide an equivalent copyable diagnostics block (if CLI is in this slice).

**Security**

- [ ] The bundle uses the same redaction as #8/#74 (Authorization/Bearer, token, secret, password, cookie, URL userinfo);
- [ ] User prompts, tool arguments/results, and full stdout/stderr files are absent;
- [ ] Log excerpts and oversized error messages are truncated to an explicit cap.

**Engineering / user journey**

- [ ] Focused unit tests and Web control tests pass;
- [ ] User-journey E2E: induce a visible error → copy → assert clipboard → assert the “file issue” link opens with a complete prefilled body. Formal E2E must not use the human trial port 4310 or its fixed data directory.

### Additional Information

Code pointers (repo-relative, for implementers; not the acceptance surface):

- `apps/web/src/Toasts.tsx`
- `apps/web/src/InlineErrorAlert.tsx`
- `apps/web/src/ErrorBoundary.tsx`
- `apps/web/src/App.tsx` (request errors go to the top-right toast)
- Data-directory logs: `.sciencediscovery-data/logs/` (`api.log` / `runner.log` / `run.log`)

issue:665.body:
Implementation: ScienceDiscovery (ours) — PR follows within 24h. Do not pick up without a `pickup-ok` comment on this issue.

Written-by: ScienceDiscovery Product
Claimed-by: ScienceDiscovery Product
Claim-Id: df196a9b36e24084ad2fcd7ec43213c4
Branch: dev/665-bug-web-fetch-public-url-guard-misses-na
Base: 80ff50c18cdf
At: 2026-10-29T04:18:18Z

Implementation: ScienceDiscovery (ours) — do not pick up without a `pickup-ok` comment.

The public-URL gate for `web_fetch` now judges IPv4-mapped (`::ffff:a.b.c.d`) and IPv4-compatible (`::a.b.c.d`) literals by their embedded IPv4 (#662 / PR #664). Three other IPv6 forms that also carry an IPv4 address still pass, so the same model-supplied URL can reach loopback or the link-local metadata endpoint through them.

Verified on live main `a074172f60e5667e72ff04246316a0a41552cb46`, `packages/data-source/src/web/native/url-guard.ts`:

- NAT64 well-known prefix `64:ff9b::/96` — the IPv4 address sits in the last two hextets. `ipv6Blocked` only consults `ipv4Blocked` for the mapped/compatible forms (`url-guard.ts:58-68` on main; `:81-97` after #664), so `64:ff9b::...` falls to `return false` (`:68` on main, `:97` after #664).
- 6to4 `2002::/16` — the IPv4 address sits in hextets 2–3, which no branch inspects.
- IPv4-translated `::ffff:0:0:0/96` — 80 zero bits, then `ffff`, then 16 zero bits, then the IPv4 address; it matches neither the mapped test (`hextets[5] === 0xffff`) nor the compatible test (first six hextets zero) that #664 added at `url-guard.ts:95-97`.
- Deprecated site-local `fec0::/10` — the link-local branch tests `fe8`–`feb` only, so `fec0::/10` is reported public.

Current vs expected: `isBlockedAddress("64:ff9b::7f00:1")`, `"2002:7f00:1::"`, `"::ffff:0:7f00:1"` and `"fec0::1"` all return `false`; each must return `true`, and `assertPublicUrl("http://[64:ff9b::7f00:1]/")` and the two siblings must reject with `local and private network URLs are not allowed`. Decoding must stay narrow: `64:ff9b::808:808`, `2002:808:808::` and `::ffff:0:808:808` embed the public `8.8.8.8` and must keep returning `false` — a fix that rejects the whole prefix breaks NAT64 and 6to4 egress.

Suggested red test, in `packages/data-source/src/web/native/providers.test.ts`, next to `web_fetch refuses private and loopback targets`:

```ts
test("web_fetch judges NAT64, 6to4 and IPv4-translated literals by their embedded IPv4", () => {
  for (const address of ["64:ff9b::7f00:1", "2002:7f00:1::", "::ffff:0:7f00:1", "fec0::1"]) {
    assert.equal(isBlockedAddress(address), true, address);
  }
  for (const address of ["64:ff9b::808:808", "2002:808:808::", "::ffff:0:808:808"]) {
    assert.equal(isBlockedAddress(address), false, address);
  }
});
```

Run: `cd packages/data-source && node --import tsx --test src/web/native/providers.test.ts`

Current result on main: 8 of 12 rows red (`false !== true` on the four blocked rows and on the three URL forms; the three public rows pass). Dedupe: open + closed search for `NAT64`, `64:ff9b`, `6to4`, `2002::`, `fec0`, `site-local`, `IPv4-translated` returns only #662/#664, which scope these classes out — no separate issue exists.

Scope note: this is the follow-up the #664 body lists as out of scope. One PR, one class of change: decode the embedded IPv4 for each of these prefixes instead of blocking the prefix.

issue:816.body:
## Problem

On native Windows, a normal chat run can terminate the API process when `SessionStore.writeArray` cannot rename a temporary state file over an existing `session-runs/<session-id>.json`. The rejected persistence operation escapes and Node exits; clients then receive `ECONNREFUSED`, and the stack supervisor cleans up the remaining services.

Confirmed by running **upstream main**, not only a downstream branch:
- Commit: `eae2cd354a366dab3796f46c9da8fe5dd7aff11c`.
- Windows 11 Pro, build 26200, x64; native Node.js 22.23.4; pnpm 11.1.2.
- Clean detached worktree; independent `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` and `pnpm build`; no product-source or lockfile changes. Startup reported Runner build `eae2cd35`.
- Native executor, real API and Runner, real managed Bash execution; a deterministic local OpenAI-compatible SSE server supplies model tool calls. No paid model, mocked filesystem, artificial lock holder, or fault injection.
- Existing managed environment and offline package cache were reused in an isolated data directory. No user data was modified.

## Reproduction

1. Check out the commit above in a native Windows checkout, install locked dependencies, and build.
2. Prepare a working managed Python/Bash starter environment through product setup. Use isolated data and ports. Existing local package caches can be reused.
3. Start the product with `node scripts/start-stack.mjs --mode local --no-build --no-jiuwenswarm`. For the client below, use API port 24410 and free model-stub port 24420; point `SCIENCE_AGENT_RUNNER_URL` at the matching isolated Runner port. Set a test-owned `SCIENCE_AGENT_AUTH_TOKEN`.
4. Save the client below as `reproduce.mjs`, set `REPRO_TOKEN` to that same test token, then run `node reproduce.mjs`. It registers a loopback model, creates sessions, submits real chat requests, polls run state, and retrieves execution output. All filesystem operations are product operations.
5. In one continuous upstream-main attempt, **10 runs completed successfully; run 11 crashed the API**. This is timing-sensitive, not a claim that every machine will fail on run 11. The prior downstream test also encountered the same failure naturally.

<details><summary>Executable reproduction client</summary>

```js
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import {createServer} from 'node:http';
import {writeFile} from 'node:fs/promises';
const base='http://127.0.0.1:24410',token=process.env.REPRO_TOKEN;
const json=async(p,b)=>{const r=await fetch(base+p,{method:b?'POST':'GET',headers:{authorization:`Bearer ${token}`,'content-type':'application/json'},...(b?{body:JSON.stringify(b)}:{})});const t=await r.text();assert.ok(r.ok,`${p}: ${r.status} ${t}`);return JSON.parse(t)};
const evidence=[],steps=[];
const commands=["set -e; printf 'c\\na\\nb\\n' > shell-input.txt; printf 'BASH_PATH='; readlink /proc/$$/exe; bash --version | head -n 1; printf 'HEAD='; head -n 1 shell-input.txt; printf 'TAIL='; tail -n 1 shell-input.txt; printf 'WC='; wc -l < shell-input.txt; printf 'SORT\\n'; sort shell-input.txt; printf 'GREP='; grep b shell-input.txt; printf 'SED\\n'; sed 's/b/B/' shell-input.txt",'windows_verification_missing_command_83971'];
let scenario=0;
const stub=createServer(async(req,res)=>{try{const chunks=[];for await(const c of req)chunks.push(c);const input=JSON.parse(Buffer.concat(chunks));const msgs=input.messages,ix=msgs.findLastIndex(m=>m.role==='user'),history=msgs.slice(ix+1),tools=history.filter(m=>m.role==='tool');evidence.push({scenario,input});let call;
if(!tools.length)call={name:'run_shell',arguments:{command:commands[scenario]}};
else {const last=String(tools.at(-1).content);let parsed;try{parsed=JSON.parse(last)}catch{};const id=parsed?.id??parsed?.execution?.id;if(id&&!/COMMAND_NOT_FOUND/.test(last)&&!/(?:completed|failed)/.test(last))call={name:'execution_status',arguments:{execution_id:id,wait_ms:10000}};}
const delta=call?{role:'assistant',tool_calls:[{index:0,id:`call_${Date.now()}`,type:'function',function:{name:call.name,arguments:JSON.stringify(call.arguments)}}]}:{role:'assistant',content:'Shell results received.'};res.writeHead(200,{'content-type':'text/event-stream'});for(const[d,f]of[[delta,null],[{},call?'tool_calls':'stop']])res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify({id:'windows',object:'chat.completion.chunk',created:1,model:'windows',choices:[{index:0,delta:d,finish_reason:f}]})}\n\n`);res.end('data: [DONE]\n\n');}catch(e){res.writeHead(500);res.end(String(e))}});
await new Promise(r=>stub.listen(24420,'127.0.0.1',r));
try{const m=await json('/api/models',{name:'Windows isolated',model:'windows',apiToken:token,baseUrl:'http://127.0.0.1:24420/v1'}),p=await json('/api/projects',{name:'Windows shell validation '+Date.now()});
for(let attempt=0;attempt<30;attempt++){for(scenario=0;scenario<commands.length;scenario++){const s=await json(`/api/projects/${p.id}/sessions`,{title:`Windows shell ${scenario}`,modelId:m.id,approvalMode:'always_allow'}),run=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/runs`,{content:`Execute Windows verification scenario ${scenario}`});let terminal;
for(let i=0;i<240;i++){const r=(await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/runs`)).find(x=>x.id===run.id);if(['completed','failed','cancelled'].includes(r.status)){terminal=r;break}await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,1000))}assert.ok(terminal,'Run timeout');assert.equal(terminal.status,'completed',JSON.stringify(terminal));
const activity=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/agent-activity`),records=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/execution-runs`);const logs=[];for(const ex of activity.executions??[]){let cursor=0;for(let n=0;n<100;n++){const page=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/agent-activity/executions/${ex.id}/logs?cursor=${cursor}`);logs.push(page);if(!page.truncated||page.nextCursor===cursor)break;cursor=page.nextCursor}};evidence.push({scenario,session:s,terminal,activity,records,logs});const output=logs.flatMap(x=>x.chunks??[]).map(x=>x.text).join('').replaceAll('\r','');
if(scenario===0){assert.ok(records.some(x=>x.exitCode===0),'successful execution required');for(const pattern of [/BASH_PATH=\/usr\/bin\/bash/,/GNU bash/,/HEAD=c/,/TAIL=b/,/WC=\s*3/,/SORT\na\nb\nc/,/GREP=b/,/SED\nc\na\nB/])assert.match(output,pattern)}steps.push({scenario,status:'PASS',runId:run.id,sessionId:s.id});}
}}finally{await writeFile('repro-evidence.json',JSON.stringify({steps,evidence},null,2));stub.closeAllConnections();stub.close()}







```

</details>

## Actual result

Observed on upstream main at 2026-11-03T13:03:01Z. Session `142e4d7f-b3a7-45dc-8468-a19e279eaa6f`, run `0ff8def7-e4a5-43d0-bf5e-c109a7b3f0a7`.

Sanitized production stack trace:

```text
Error: EPERM: operation not permitted, rename
  <data>/session-runs/.state-37512-ae6a0cf6-4653-49d3-ae81-cfccb1e4f462.tmp
  -> <data>/session-runs/142e4d7f-b3a7-45dc-8468-a19e279eaa6f.json
    at async rename (node:internal/fs/promises:784:10)
    at async SessionStore.writeArray (<checkout>/services/api/dist/store.js:1829:13)
    at async <checkout>/services/api/dist/store.js:1840:13
errno: -4048
code: EPERM
syscall: rename
Node.js v22.23.4
```

The client subsequently fails with `ECONNREFUSED`; the API is no longer listening.

## Expected result

Transient Windows state-file replacement conflicts should be handled without terminating the API. If persistence cannot recover, the affected run should report an actionable error and the service should remain available, with run state recoverable.

## Investigation and limits

`services/api/src/store.ts` implements `writeArray` as temporary-file `writeFile` followed by a direct `rename`, with temporary-file cleanup in `finally`. Its mutation queue serializes writes to one path, but the replacement has no Windows retry behavior. Upstream already provides a retrying `renameReplace` in `services/api/src/platform/os/win32.ts` (related platform work: #637); this particular persistence path does not use it.

The exact conflicting file handle has **not** been identified. Antivirus, indexing, or overlapping readers are hypotheses, not established causes. The observed facts are the real `EPERM` replacement failure and the unhandled error terminating the production API. No product fix is included in this report.

issue:867.body:
## Checklist

- [x] I have searched for related issues but did not get the expected help.
- [x] This bug has not been fixed in the latest version.
- [x] The reproduction below is minimal, self-contained, and includes the observed result.

## 🐞 Detailed Description of the Problem

**Current:** a workspace file whose size is just under a unit boundary is printed with the *next* unit's number still attached to the *previous* unit name. A file of `1_048_575` bytes (1 MiB − 1) reads **`1024.0 KiB`** in the Workspace file provenance modal — a number no KiB value can have. The CSV artifact inspector has the same boundary: `1_048_575` bytes reads **`1024.0 KiB`**, and `1_073_741_823` bytes (1 GiB − 1) reads **`1024.0 MiB`**.

**Expected:** a size never prints a value at or above the unit's own base. A file one byte under 1 MiB is `1.0 MiB` (or, for a truncating formatter, `1023.9 KiB`) — never `1024.0 KiB`. Today the reader is told the file is ~1024 KiB *and* still labelled KiB, which is both off by a unit and internally contradictory; in a provenance record the size is the only quantitative evidence the user has that the file is the one they think it is.

### Where

`apps/web/src/WorkspaceFileProvenanceModal.tsx:39-49` (read on `main` `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0`, blob `e5eafffec133`):

```ts
function formatBytes(bytes: number): string {
  if (bytes < 1_024) return `${bytes} B`;
  const units = ["KiB", "MiB", "GiB", "TiB"];
  let value = bytes / 1_024;
  let unit = units[0]!;
  for (let index = 1; index < units.length && value >= 1_024; index += 1) {
    value /= 1_024;
    unit = units[index]!;
  }
  return `${value.toFixed(value >= 10 ? 1 : 2)} ${unit}`;
}
```

The loop promotes only while the *unrounded* value is ≥ 1024, but the printed value is the *rounded* one: `1_048_575 / 1_024 = 1023.999…`, the loop stops at KiB, and `toFixed(1)` then rounds up to `1024.0`. Sizes are shown from this function at `:122` (`currentRevision.size`) and `:162` (every revision in the revision list).

The CSV artifact inspector has the same shape, and additionally never leaves MiB, in `apps/web/src/csv-artifact/csv/inferCsv.ts:389-393` (blob `e34bd7458b1e`), reached from `apps/web/src/csv-artifact/components/InspectorPanel.tsx:24`:

```ts
export function formatBytes(bytes: number): string {
  if (bytes < 1024) return `${bytes} B`;
  if (bytes < 1024 ** 2) return `${(bytes / 1024).toFixed(1)} KiB`;
  return `${(bytes / 1024 ** 2).toFixed(1)} MiB`;
}
```

Measured on `main` `89cd2d30` (values printed by both formatters):

| bytes | provenance modal | CSV inspector |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `1_048_574` | `1024.0 KiB` | `1024.0 KiB` |
| `1_048_575` | `1024.0 KiB` | `1024.0 KiB` |
| `1_073_741_823` | `1024.0 MiB` | `1024.0 MiB` |
| `1_099_511_627_775` | `1024.0 GiB` | `1048576.0 MiB` |

### Red-first reproduction (fails on `main` `89cd2d30`)

```tsx
// apps/web/tests/sizeBoundary.test.tsx — provenance side
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { createElement } from "react";
import { renderToStaticMarkup } from "react-dom/server";
import type { WorkspaceFileProvenance } from "@sciencediscovery/schema";
import { WorkspaceFileProvenanceModal } from "../src/WorkspaceFileProvenanceModal.js";

const ts = "2026-08-01T10:00:00.000Z";
const size = 1_048_575; // one byte under 1 MiB
const revision = {
  artifactVersionIds: [], contentHash: "a".repeat(64), createdAt: ts, fileId: "file-1",
  id: "revision-1", modifiedAt: ts, origin: "tool" as const, path: "results/report.md",
  projectId: "project-1", runId: "run-1", sessionId: "session-1", size, toolName: "run_python" as const,
};
const provenance = {
  artifacts: [], currentRevision: revision, lineage: [], revisions: [revision],
  file: { createdAt: ts, currentRevisionId: revision.id, id: revision.fileId, path: revision.path,
    projectId: revision.projectId, sessionId: revision.sessionId, sessionTitle: "S", updatedAt: ts },
  sourceSession: { deleted: false, id: "session-1", title: "S" },
} as unknown as WorkspaceFileProvenance;

const html = renderToStaticMarkup(createElement(WorkspaceFileProvenanceModal, {
  file: { modifiedAt: ts, path: revision.path, size }, onClose: () => {}, provenance,
}));
assert.ok(!/1024\.0 KiB/.test(html), "a KiB value must stay under 1024");
assert.match(html, /1\.0(0)? MiB/);
```

```ts
// apps/web/tests/csvSizeBoundary.test.ts
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { formatBytes } from "../src/csv-artifact/csv/inferCsv.js";

assert.equal(formatBytes(1_048_575), "1.0 MiB");
assert.equal(formatBytes(1_073_741_823), "1.0 GiB");
assert.equal(formatBytes(3 * 1024 ** 3), "3.0 GiB");
```

Observed on `main` `89cd2d30` with `node --import tsx --test tests/sizeBoundary.test.tsx`:

```
# PROVENANCE_SIZES ["1024.0 KiB","1024.0 KiB"]
# CSV_INSPECTOR_SIZE 1024.0 KiB
# AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: a file one byte under 1 MiB must read 1.00 MiB
# pass 0 # fail 1
```

**Suggested fix (one PR):** promote to the next unit when the *rounded* value reaches the base (compare `value.toFixed(decimals)` against `1_024` before choosing the unit, or round half-down/floor before the threshold test), and give the CSV formatter the same KiB → MiB → GiB → TiB ladder the provenance formatter already has. Both are pure functions with no data dependency. The same quantity is already formatted by three different functions in `apps/web` (this one, the CSV inspector, and `apps/web/src/RuntimeControls.tsx:141-146`, which falls back to a raw byte count when the size is not an exact GiB/MiB multiple), so one shared, unit-correct helper is the smallest fix that stops the three from disagreeing.

## Environment

- `main` `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0`
- `node v22.23.2`, `pnpm 11.1.2`, Linux x64
- Reproduce: `cd apps/web && node --import tsx --test tests/sizeBoundary.test.tsx`

## Version Information

- ScienceDiscovery `main` @ `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0` (2026-11-04)
- Affected surfaces: Workspace file provenance modal (size + revision list), CSV artifact inspector header

issue:871.body:
## Checklist

- [x] I have searched for related issues but did not get the expected help.
- [x] This bug has not been fixed in the latest version.
- [x] The reproduction below is minimal, self-contained, and includes the observed result.

## 🐞 Detailed Description of the Problem

**Current:** with the UI language set to Simplified Chinese, cancelling a queued run answers in English. The action itself is translated — the Runs panel says `排队中的运行` / `取消排队中的运行` — but the toast the app pushes with its result is a hardcoded English sentence, and the same is true for the three other outcomes:

- cancel succeeded → **`Queued run cancelled`**
- the run already started → title **`Run already started`**, detail **`The queued run has already started; use Stop to cancel the active run.`** (which also names the English button `Stop`)
- the run already finished → **`Run already finished`**
- any other status → title **`Queued run updated`**, detail **`Current status: <status>`**

**Expected:** the toast is translated like every other outcome the user hears about (the neighbouring `error.cancelQueuedRun` key exists in both locales), so a Chinese user reads the result of the button they pressed in Chinese.

### Where

`apps/web/src/run-stream/model.ts:130-142` (read on `main` `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0`, blob `74c972ea11ad`):

```ts
export function queuedCancelToast(status: SessionRun["status"]): { detail?: string; title: string; tone: "error" | "info" } {
  if (status === "cancelled") return { title: "Queued run cancelled", tone: "info" };
  if (isActiveRunStatus(status)) {
    return {
      detail: "The queued run has already started; use Stop to cancel the active run.",
      title: "Run already started",
      tone: "info",
    };
  }
  if (isTerminalRunStatus(status)) return { title: "Run already finished", tone: "info" };
  return { detail: `Current status: ${status}`, title: "Queued run updated", tone: "info" };
}
```

Nothing in the function can reach a translator, and the toast is pushed verbatim at `apps/web/src/App.tsx:3802-3803`:

```ts
const toast = queuedCancelToast(cancelled.status);
pushToast(toast.tone, toast.title, toast.detail);
```

`apps/web/src/i18n/messages.ts` carries the *action* keys (`app.queuedRuns`, `app.queued`, `app.cancelQueuedRun`) and the *failure* key (`error.cancelQueuedRun`) in both locales, but no key for any of these four outcomes — so there is nothing for the effect to reuse.

The reason this is not the closed localization sweep is visible in history: `4f3b802f fix(web): localize run failure guidance and collapse diagnostics` (2026-10-25) is the last commit that touched this file and localized the failure guidance next to these lines, and the English text is *pinned by a test* — `apps/web/tests/ComposerRunAction.test.tsx:195-201` asserts `{ title: "Queued run cancelled" }`, `"Run already started"`, `"Run already finished"`. Any sweep that only greps the source would have seen that test as proof the strings were intended.

### Red-first reproduction (fails on `main` `89cd2d30`)

```ts
// apps/web/tests/queuedCancelToast.test.ts
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { queuedCancelToast } from "../src/run-stream/model.js";
import { zhCN } from "../src/i18n/messages.js";

const asked: string[] = [];
const t = (key: string) => { asked.push(key); return "翻译后的文案"; };

queuedCancelToast("cancelled", t);           // the fixed signature passes the translator in
assert.ok(asked.length > 0, "the cancel outcome must go through the message catalog");
assert.ok(Object.keys(zhCN).some((key) => key.startsWith("app.queuedCancelOutcome")));
```

Observed on `main` `89cd2d30` with `cd apps/web && node --import tsx --test tests/queuedCancelToast.test.ts`:

```
# CANCELLED_TOAST {"title":"Queued run cancelled","tone":"info"}
# TRANSLATOR_CALLS 0
# AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: the cancel outcome must go through the message catalog
# pass 0 # fail 1
```

**Suggested fix (one PR):** give `queuedCancelToast` the translator (`(status, t)`) and return `t(...)` for the four titles and two details, add the keys to `en` and `zhCN`, and update the three assertions in `apps/web/tests/ComposerRunAction.test.tsx` to compare message keys rather than English literals so the next sweep cannot re-pin them. Six strings, one pure function, no data dependency.

## Environment

- `main` `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0`
- `node v22.23.2`, `pnpm 11.1.2`, Linux x64
- UI language set to `中文 (简体)`; Runs panel → a queued run → `取消排队中的运行`
- Reproduce: `cd apps/web && node --import tsx --test tests/queuedCancelToast.test.ts`

## Version Information

- ScienceDiscovery `main` @ `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0` (2026-11-04)
- Affected surface: composer Runs panel → cancel a queued run (toast), both web and desktop shells

issue:877.body:
## Checklist

- [x] I have searched for related issues but did not get the expected help.
- [x] This bug has not been fixed in the latest version.
- [x] The reproduction below is minimal, self-contained, and includes the observed result.

## 🐞 Detailed Description of the Problem

**Current:** asking for the provenance or the diff of an artifact version that does not exist answers **500 `{"error":"Internal server error"}`** and writes an `unclassified_request_error` line to the API log. The sibling route for the same missing version answers **404 `Artifact version not found`**:

```
GET /api/sessions/<session>/artifact-versions/<missing>/content     → 404 {"error":"Artifact version not found"}
GET /api/sessions/<session>/artifact-versions/<missing>/provenance  → 500 {"error":"Internal server error"}
GET /api/sessions/<session>/artifact-versions/<missing>/diff        → 500 {"error":"Internal server error"}
```

**Expected:** a version id that does not exist is a client error on every artifact route — 404 with the same body the `content` route returns — so the Artifact modal can say the version is gone instead of showing "Internal server error", and the server log stops reporting a server fault for a plain bad id.

### Where

`services/api/src/http/index.ts` (read on `main` `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0`, blob `910ca85a4ae3`). The `content` route checks the version first:

```ts
// :3353-3359
const artifactVersionContentMatch = url.pathname.match(/^\/api\/sessions\/([^/]+)\/artifact-versions\/([^/]+)\/content$/);
if (artifactVersionContentMatch && request.method === "GET") {
  const version = store.getArtifactVersion(artifactVersionContentMatch[1]!, artifactVersionContentMatch[2]!);
  if (!version) return sendError(response, 404, "Artifact version not found");
  ...
```

but the `provenance` and `diff` routes (`:3360-3381`) call their helpers directly, with no existence check:

```ts
if (artifactVersionProvenanceMatch && request.method === "GET") {
  sendJson(response, 200, await artifactVersionProvenance(store, provenanceRecorder, memoryGraphClient,
    artifactVersionProvenanceMatch[1]!, artifactVersionProvenanceMatch[2]!));
```

Those helpers throw a plain `Error`, not an `ApiStatusError` and not a `SessionStoreHttpError`:

- `services/api/src/artifacts/index.ts:169` — `if (!version) throw new Error("Artifact version not found");` (blob `2d9539265751`)
- `services/api/src/artifacts/index.ts:349` — `if (!to) throw new Error("Artifact version not found");` (the `?against=` id on the diff route)

Nothing downstream recognises that message, so the top-level catch falls through to its last branch: `isKnownClientInputError` (`services/api/src/http/error-classification.ts:20`, blob `38b0b962dac9`) only matches `is required` / `must` / `unknown value` / `cannot` / `does not match` / `invalid` / `unsafe … path|archive|package` / `plain basename`, and the 404 message regex in `http/index.ts:3924` only covers `Project|Session|Proxy server|Provider|Model not found` and `Execution|Transfer|Timer|Subagent not found: …`. `Artifact version not found` matches neither → 500 `Internal server error` + `apiLog.error("unclassified_request_error", …)`.

User impact: a stale artifact link, a version deleted by retention, or a diff against a version that no longer exists is reported as a server failure. The Artifact modal reads content and provenance together (`apps/web/src/ScientificArtifacts.tsx:686`), so this is also the status a reader sees when the version is simply gone.

### Red-first reproduction (fails on `main` `89cd2d30`)

Real HTTP server, no mocks:

```ts
// services/api/src/artifactRouteStatus.test.ts
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { randomUUID } from "node:crypto";
import { mkdir, mkdtemp, rm } from "node:fs/promises";
import { resolve } from "node:path";
import { closeApiServer, createApiServer, loadServerConfig } from "./http/index.js";

const root = resolve(process.cwd(), ".tmp"); await mkdir(root, { recursive: true });
const dir = await mkdtemp(resolve(root, "artifact-route-"));
const config = loadServerConfig({
  SCIENCE_AGENT_DATA_DIR: dir, SCIENCE_AGENT_AUTH_TOKEN: "test-local-credential",
  SCIENCE_AGENT_RUNNER_TOKEN: "test-runner-credential", SCIENCE_AGENT_RUNNER_URL: "http://127.0.0.1:1",
  SCIENCE_AGENT_PORT: "0", SCIENCE_AGENT_MODEL_CATALOG_URL: "",
});
const server = createApiServer(config);
await new Promise<void>((done) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", done));
const origin = `http://127.0.0.1:${(server.address() as { port: number }).port}`;
const headers = { authorization: "Bearer test-local-credential" };
const missing = `api/sessions/${randomUUID()}/artifact-versions/${randomUUID()}`;
try {
  const status = async (route: string) => (await fetch(`${origin}/${missing}/${route}`, { headers })).status;
  assert.equal(await status("content"), 404);
  assert.equal(await status("provenance"), 404, "a missing version is a client error");
  assert.equal(await status("diff"), 404, "a missing version is a client error");
} finally { await closeApiServer(server); await rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true, maxRetries: 5 }); }
```

Observed on `main` `89cd2d30` with `cd services/api && node --import tsx --test src/artifactRouteStatus.test.ts`:

```
# CONTENT_STATUS 404
# PROVENANCE_STATUS 500 "{\"error\":\"Internal server error\"}\n"
# DIFF_STATUS 500 "{\"error\":\"Internal server error\"}\n"
# STATUSES {"content":404,"provenance":500,"diff":500}
# AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: a missing version is a client error
# pass 0 # fail 1
```

**Suggested fix (one PR):** resolve the version once in each route and answer 404 like the `content` route does (`:3355-3356`), for both the `:versionId` and the `?against=` id on the diff route; alternatively add `Artifact (version )?not found` to the 404 message regex, but the route-level check is smaller and matches the sibling route. One test on the real server covers all three routes.

## Environment

- `main` `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0`
- `node v22.23.2`, `pnpm 11.1.2`, Linux x64
- Reproduce: `cd services/api && node --import tsx --test src/artifactRouteStatus.test.ts`
- Observed in the API log: `event=unclassified_request_error … status=500` for a request that only named a version that does not exist

## Version Information

- ScienceDiscovery `main` @ `89cd2d30c93e11c6e98e264cc08b171f6a3705f0` (2026-11-04)
- Affected endpoints: `GET /api/sessions/:sessionId/artifact-versions/:versionId/provenance`, `GET /api/sessions/:sessionId/artifact-versions/:versionId/diff`

closed_recent.body:
## 1. Summary

The Evolve LLM proxy writes one usage row per proxied completion, which is correct. But it derives the row's identity from the **run** (`invocationId: evolve:<runId>`) while holding `attemptIndex` at a constant `0`. The ledger's idempotency key is `(sessionId, invocationId, invocationKind, attemptIndex)`, so for a run that makes N model calls the first row is written and calls 2..N are discarded as if they were replays of call 1. Nothing is logged on that path, so the loss is invisible.

Measured on a real environment: a 73-call research recorded **0.83 %** of its tokens, and an 85-call research recorded **0.93 %**.

## 2. Expected behaviour

The repository already pins this contract in its own tests. `(invocationId, attemptIndex)` is the **attempt axis of one logical call**, not the identity of a run:

```ts
// services/api/src/store.test.ts:1184
test("SessionStore ignores duplicate model usage attempts but keeps retries", …
// :1220  await store.appendModelInvocationUsage(record);
// :1221  await store.appendModelInvocationUsage({ …record, id: "usage-duplicate", totalTokens: 30 });
// :1222  await store.appendModelInvocationUsage({ …record, attemptIndex: 1, id: "usage-retry", totalTokens: 4 });
// :1225  assert.deepEqual(records.map((usage) => usage.id).toSorted(), ["usage-0", "usage-retry"]);
// :1226  assert.deepEqual(records.map((usage) => usage.attemptIndex).toSorted(), [0, 1]);

// services/api/src/model-usage.test.ts:83
test("USG-005 invocation attempts keep a stable invocationId and increment attemptIndex", …
```

So: two rows that share all four key fields **must** be the same attempt of the same call, and a genuinely new call must present either a new `invocationId` or a higher `attemptIndex`. Distinct calls that differ only in their row `id` (`:259`, `randomBytes(16)`) are, by this contract, not distinguishable.

The same file already contains a writer that satisfies the contract for the very same `invocationKind`:

```ts
// services/api/src/evolution/direction-draft.ts:58-63
const id = randomUUID();
await store.appendModelInvocationUsage({
  id, invocationId: id, invocationKind: "evolve", attemptIndex: 0,
  outcome: draft ? "completed" : "failed", …
```

## 3. Actual behaviour

One row per run. Everything after the first completion is dropped in silence.

## 4. Source code

All paths relative to the repository root, quoted at main `f0efd382e`.

### 4.1 The writer builds a run-scoped key with a constant attempt index

```ts
// services/api/src/evolution/llm-proxy.ts
:232    try {
:233      const usage = await recordUsage(deps, grant, model, payload, startedAt);
:234      deps.onUsage?.(usage);
:235    } catch (error) {
:236      apiLog.warn("evolve_llm_usage_unrecorded", {          // only fires on a thrown error
:245  async function recordUsage(
:252    const reported = parseProviderUsage(payload.usage, "openai-chat-completions");
:253    const record: ModelInvocationUsage = {
:254      attemptIndex: 0,                                      // constant for every call of the run
:259      id: randomBytes(16).toString("hex"),                  // unique row id — not part of the key
:261      invocationId: `evolve:${grant.runId}`,                // one key per run
:264      invocationKind: "evolve",
:277    await deps.store.appendModelInvocationUsage(record);
```

### 4.2 The ledger drops the second call without a trace

```ts
// services/api/src/store.ts
:7634   async appendModelInvocationUsage(usage: ModelInvocationUsage): Promise<void> {
:7640     if (records.some((record) =>
:7641       record.sessionId === usage.sessionId
:7642       && record.invocationId === usage.invocationId
:7643       && record.invocationKind === usage.invocationKind
:7644       && record.attemptIndex === usage.attemptIndex)) {
:7645       return;                                            // silent: no log, no counter, no signal
:7647     records.push(this.normalizeModelInvocationUsage(usage));
:7652   async getSessionUsageSummary(sessionId: string) { … }  // summariseModelUsage counts rows as calls
```

The early `return` at `:7645` is not an error, so `llm-proxy.ts:236`'s `evolve_llm_usage_unrecorded` warning cannot fire for the calls this drops.

## 5. Reproduction

### 5.1 Observed on a real run (read-only inspection of an existing data volume)

Environment: a running ScienceDiscovery stack, Evolve search driven through the normal `/idea-tree` research path, one session, provider usage reported on every call.

| Evolve run | Proxied calls (proxy log) | Σ tokens at the proxy | Ledger rows for that `runId` | Tokens in the ledger | Share recorded |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `research-2864a8e9-ab07-4c8a-9d33-0be6f17639c3` | 73 (73 × HTTP 200, 0 non-200) | 1,752,397 (in 1,282,961 / out 469,436) | **1** | 14,621 | **0.8343 %** |
